\section{What a Greedy Rewrite Promises}

A practical selector can rank its matching rules, try them in order, and commit to the first legal one. Blindell's account of LLVM's SelectionDAG selector describes an historical implementation: TableGen expands the instruction descriptions into tree patterns, and the matcher generator orders them lexicographically by decreasing pattern complexity, increasing cost, and increasing size of the replacement graph. Complexity includes a priority adjustment, so it is not merely the number of nodes. The ordered tests become a matcher table. A successful rewrite replaces part of the program DAG and changes the choices at later nodes \cite[chapter~4]{blindell2016instruction}.

The ordering is a strategy for selection. The matcher can correctly recognize every candidate in a toy region and still return inferior code if it commits without considering the alternatives. In an implementation that interleaves matching and selection, this distinction helps debugging: log whether the missed instruction failed a test or was made unavailable by an earlier rewrite. The survey's description of one LLVM version does not imply that every modern target uses the same patterns, priorities, or multi-result support.

A useful invariant for a greedy rewrite is that all uses outside the removed operations still have definitions. Before committing, inventory the outgoing uses, including live-outs of the region. Redirect only those that the replacement can supply with the correct meaning and operand class. If an internal result is still needed and not exported, either reject the rewrite or model a separate computation where that is legal. Also preserve required ordering edges: finding an opcode match is no license to hide an ordering constraint. A deterministic priority scheme makes a poor choice reproducible, but reproducibility is not evidence of optimality.

\section{Local Savings Do Not Add Across Overlaps}

Consider a toy target with pure eight-bit operations. The input $x$ is already available. Let $u$ be a one-bit left rotation of $x$. One arm computes $v=u\mathbin{\&}15$, $w=v\mathbin{\mathtt{xor}}3$, and $y=w+1$ modulo 256; the other computes $p=u\gg2$ with zero fill and $q=p\mathbin{\mathtt{xor}}15$. Both $y$ and $q$ are live-outs. Singletons exist for all operations: $u,p,q$ cost three each, and $v,w,y$ cost two each. They produce ordinary registers.

In addition, candidate $A$ owns $u,v,w,y$, exports $u,y$, and costs three. Candidate $B$ takes $u$ as input, owns $v,w,y$, exports $y$, and costs two. Candidate $C$ owns $u,p,q$, exports $u,q$, and costs four. These are all the non-singleton candidates. We require exact ownership, and each candidate handles its own internal dependencies. The exported $u$ makes both $A$ and $C$ legal on this DAG, but they cannot both be selected. $B$ and $C$ can coexist: their only connection is the ordinary register $u$ from $C$ to $B$.

Relative to the all-singleton baseline, $A$ saves six units, $B$ saves four, and $C$ saves five. A rule that always takes the largest remaining match chooses $A$ first. So does a rule that takes the largest immediate singleton saving. Both ignore the fact that $A$ blocks two candidates that might otherwise be used together. A first-fit root traversal can also go either way: with a preference for the largest match at a root, visiting $y$ first can commit $A$, whereas visiting $q$ first can commit $C$ and leave $B$ available at $y$. The example isolates the commitment error: both outcomes use candidates from the same list.

\Cref{fig:visual-272} separates the shared data dependence from the match conflicts that drive the greedy decision.

